y:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:760px}</style><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Upkeep and insurance are the biggest parts. "Besides the loan" means everything except paying back the loan and its interest. Your lender usually collects the tax and insurance with each loan payment, but they are counted here, not in the loan. The HOA fee and upkeep are usually paid separately.</p><div class="c3k-bill"><table><caption>A typical Horry County house, about $350,000, outside city limits: what it costs a year besides the loan</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">What you pay</th><th scope="col" class="n">Your home</th><th scope="col" class="n">Second home</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Property tax<small>the rate is charged on 4 percent of the value (6 percent for a second home)</small></th><td class="n">$1,290</td><td class="n">$4,220</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">County stormwater fee<small>on most tax bills outside city limits</small></th><td class="n">$89</td><td class="n">$89</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Home insurance<small>what owners of a house here pay for all their policies</small></th><td class="n">$2,000</td><td class="n">$2,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">HOA fee<small>a typical house for sale, $100 a month</small></th><td class="n">$1,200</td><td class="n">$1,200</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Upkeep<small>routine repairs plus the big replacements</small></th><td class="n">$3,270</td><td class="n">$3,270</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><th scope="row">Total a year</th><td class="n">$7,850</td><td class="n">$10,780</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A month</th><td class="n">$650</td><td class="n">$900</td></tr></tfoot></table></div><p class="c3k-note">Not in the total: electricity and water, about $2,280 a year. A home with a loan in the high-risk flood zone also needs a flood policy (see insurance below). The second-home column changes only the tax. Insurance on a second home can cost more.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/carolina-forest-house-streets-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/carolina-forest-house-streets-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/carolina-forest-house-streets-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Rows of two-story houses with gray roofs and short driveways along curving streets and a cul-de-sac, beside ponds and pine woods, seen from above" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses on streets in Carolina Forest, outside city limits, seen from above. The typical house in the table above is one like these.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, the same house costs about $7,890 a year as your home. The tax is higher there, about $1,410, and there is no county fee on the tax bill.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does a house cost to own in each town?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Between about $7,600 and $8,300 a year for a typical house, as your home. North Myrtle Beach has the highest prices, but most of its houses for sale list no HOA fee.</p><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>A typical house in five places, as your home: what it costs to own</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Place</th><th scope="col" class="n">Typical price</th><th scope="col" class="n">Tax and fee</th><th scope="col">HOA fee a month</th><th scope="col" class="n">A year (a month)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Socastee</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$292,000</td><td class="n" data-label="Tax and fee">$1,160</td><td data-label="HOA fee a month">$100</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$7,640 ($640)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">North Myrtle Beach</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$535,000</td><td class="n" data-label="Tax and fee">$2,400</td><td data-label="HOA fee a month">none listed</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$7,670 ($640)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Conway</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$265,000</td><td class="n" data-label="Tax and fee">$1,730</td><td data-label="HOA fee a month">$75</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$7,910 ($660)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">City of Myrtle Beach</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$430,000</td><td class="n" data-label="Tax and fee">$1,740</td><td data-label="HOA fee a month">$100</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$8,210 ($680)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Carolina Forest</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$395,000</td><td class="n" data-label="Tax and fee">$1,540</td><td data-label="HOA fee a month">$125</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$8,310 ($690)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class="c3k-note c3k-nl"><li>Carolina Forest and Socastee are outside city limits, where most tax bills include the $89 county fee.</li><li>The city rows leave out each city's own stormwater and trash fees, which come with the water bill.</li><li>On the north end of Myrtle Beach, a typical house lists an HOA fee of $300 a month. Conway's HOA fee is for its main ZIP code, which is mostly outside the city.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Prices differ from town to town far more than these costs do. Our <a href="/buyers/affordable-places-to-live/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">affordable places page</a> shows where a typical home costs least.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="condo-or-house"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Condo or house">Is a condo cheaper to own than a house in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Carolina Forest, yes. Its condo fees are the lowest in the table below, and a typical condo there costs about $1,000 a year less to own than a typical house. In Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach, no: their condo fees are the highest.</p><figure class="c3k-pair c3k-diff" role="group" aria-label="How much more a typical condo or house costs to own a year, as your home"><span class="cap">How much more a year the condo or the house costs to own, as your home</span><div class="key"><span><span class="sw" style="background:#c4783a" aria-hidden="true"></span>The condo costs more</span><span><span class="sw" style="background:#5c6b78" aria-hidden="true"></span>The house costs more</span></div><div class="g"><span class="lab"><span>City of Myrtle Beach</span><b>condo costs more</b></span><span class="c3k-sr" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)">The condo costs about $3,280 more a year.</span><div class="r" aria-hidden="true"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:91.9%;background:#c4783a"></span></span><span class="v">$3,280 more</span></div></div><div class="g"><span class="lab"><span>North Myrtle Beach</span><b>condo costs more</b></span><span class="c3k-sr" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)">The condo costs about $3,570 more a year.</span><div class="r" aria-hidden="true"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:100.0%;background:#c4783a"></span></span><span class="v">$3,570 more</span></div></div><div class="g"><span class="lab"><span>Carolina Forest</span><b>house costs more</b></span><span class="c3k-sr" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)">The house costs about $1,000 more a year.</span><div class="r" aria-hidden="true"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:28.1%;background:#5c6b78"></span></span><span class="v">$1,000 more</span></div></div><figcaption>A typical condo against a typical house in the same place: tax, the county fee where it applies, insurance, the HOA or condo fee and upkeep. The totals are in the town tables.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The condo is cheaper to buy, so its loan is smaller. In Myrtle Beach a typical condo costs less than half as much as a typical house; in North Myrtle Beach, a little more than half.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Why are condo fees so high in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">They are highest near the ocean in Myrtle Beach. On the city's north end, a typical condo for sale lists about $875 a month. In Carolina Forest, inland, condos list about half that.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Owners pay the fee for everything they share: running the building, upkeep, and improvements to the grounds, halls, lobby, parking and pool. It may also include the tax and insurance on the shared property. Your own unit's insurance is separate: condo owners pay about $800 a year for it.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-condos-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-condos-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-condos-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Oceanfront condo buildings in Myrtle Beach seen from the ocean: a white high-rise tower and three low white-roofed buildings around pools and parking lots, behind the dunes and the beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: USGS. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: USGS</a></span></div><figcaption>Condo buildings on the ocean in Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane. Condo owners share the cost of grounds, pools and parking lots like these through the condo fee.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In each place below, the condo fee is about three quarters of what a condo owner pays a year to own the unit.</p><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>A typical condo in five places, as your home: what it costs to own</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Place</th><th scope="col" class="n">Typical price</th><th scope="col">Condo fee a month</th><th scope="col" class="n">A year (a month)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">City of Myrtle Beach</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$172,000</td><td data-label="Condo fee a month">$775</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$11,490 ($960)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">North Myrtle Beach</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$300,000</td><td data-label="Condo fee a month">$700</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$11,240 ($940)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Garden City</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$220,000</td><td data-label="Condo fee a month">$525</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$8,750 ($730)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Little River</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$155,000</td><td data-label="Condo fee a month">$525</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$8,450 ($700)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Carolina Forest</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical price">$172,000</td><td data-label="Condo fee a month">$425</td><td class="n" data-label="A year (a month)">$7,310 ($610)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class="c3k-note c3k-nl"><li>Each total is the condo fee plus the owner's own tax, insurance and upkeep, and the county fee outside city limits.</li><li>Garden City's condo fee is for its ZIP code, which also covers Murrells Inlet.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to the building's policy and your own</a> shows what each one covers. When a building needs more than its savings, owners get a <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">special assessment</a>. Whether a condo pays as an investment is on our <a href="/invest/condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condo investing page</a>. Why some buildings cannot get a normal loan is on our <a href="/invest/non-warrantable-condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">page on condos most lenders will not lend on</a>.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><h3 style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .4rem">Looking at a condo to rent out?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Some communities ban renting at all. Our agents read the HOA documents for rules that would block your plan before you offer.</p><p style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.6rem;margin:0"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/?topic=cost-to-own">Talk to a specialized agent</a><a class="btn btn-brass" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></p></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How many homes here pay an HOA fee?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More than half. About 6 in 10 homeowners across most of Horry County pay an HOA or condo fee; in the rest of South Carolina, about 1 in 3. Still, more than 1 in 4 houses for sale here list no HOA fee. Our page on <a href="/buyers/no-hoa-homes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">homes with no HOA</a> shows where they are.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="second-home-tax"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Second home tax">How much more does a second home pay in property tax?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 2.5 to 3.8 times as much as the same house as your home. On a $350,000 house, that is about $2,900 to $3,900 more a year, depending on the town.</p><figure class="c3k-pair" role="group" aria-label="Property tax a year on the same house, valued at $350,000"><span class="cap">Property tax a year on the same house, valued at $350,000</span><div class="key"><span><span class="sw" style="background:#c4783a" aria-hidden="true"></span>As your home (4 percent)</span><span><span class="sw" style="background:#5c6b78" aria-hidden="true"></span>As a second home (6 percent)</span></div><div class="g"><span class="lab"><span>City of Myrtle Beach</span><b>3.8 times</b></span><span class="c3k-sr" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)">As your home (4 percent): $1,410. As a second home (6 percent): $5,350.</span><div aria-hidden="true"><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:24.7%;background:#c4783a"></span></span><span class="v">$1,410</span></div><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:93.4%;background:#5c6b78"></span></span><span class="v">$5,350</span></div></div></div><div class="g"><span class="lab"><span>Garden City and Murrells Inlet, Horry side (most homes)</span><b>3.2 times</b></span><span class="c3k-sr" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)">As your home (4 percent): $1,380. As a second home (6 percent): $4,350.</span><div aria-hidden="true"><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:24.0%;background:#c4783a"></span></span><span class="v">$1,380</span></div><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:76.1%;background:#5c6b78"></span></span><span class="v">$4,350</span></div></div></div><div class="g"><span class="lab"><span>Surfside Beach (in the town)</span><b>3.1 times</b></span><span class="c3k-sr" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)">As your home (4 percent): $1,470. As a second home (6 percent): $4,500.</span><div aria-hidden="true"><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:25.7%;background:#c4783a"></span></span><span class="v">$1,470</span></div><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:78.6%;background:#5c6b78"></span></span><span class="v">$4,500</span></div></div></div><div class="g"><span class="lab"><span>North Myrtle Beach</span><b>3 times</b></span><span class="c3k-sr" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)">As your home (4 percent): $1,570. As a second home (6 percent): $4,650.</span><div aria-hidden="true"><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:27.4%;background:#c4783a"></span></span><span class="v">$1,570</span></div><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:81.2%;background:#5c6b78"></span></span><span class="v">$4,650</span></div></div></div><div class="g"><span class="lab"><span>Conway</span><b>2.5 times</b></span><span class="c3k-sr" style="position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)">As your home (4 percent): $2,290. As a second home (6 percent): $5,720.</span><div aria-hidden="true"><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:40.0%;background:#c4783a"></span></span><span class="v">$2,290</span></div><div class="r"><span class="t"><span class="b" style="width:100.0%;background:#5c6b78"></span></span><span class="v">$5,720</span></div></div></div><figcaption>Outside city limits, the tax is in the table at the top. Horry County's current tax rates, with the City of Myrtle Beach credit for a home taxed at 4 percent. Tax only: the county's stormwater fee and small special districts are left out.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On a home you live in, the tax rate is charged on 4 percent of its value, not the whole value: on a $350,000 house, on $14,000. It also pays only part of the school tax. A second home or a rental is charged on 6 percent and pays all of the school tax.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The 4 percent rate is not automatic: you apply for it. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax guide</a> has the steps, and our <a href="/buyers/common-mistakes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">buyer mistakes page</a> shows what paying 6 percent by mistake costs.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At 65 you can have part of your home's value exempted from property tax. Our <a href="/buyers/retirees/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">retiree guide</a> covers who qualifies and how much.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="insurance"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Insurance">What does homeowners insurance cost in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A typical house owner in Horry County pays about $2,000 a year, about 40 percent more than in the rest of South Carolina. That is all their home insurance together, with a wind or flood policy included where they have one. Half pay between about $1,300 and $3,000.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Near the ocean, wind can be a separate policy. On the ocean side of U.S. 17, the highway along the coast, an insurer may leave wind and hail out of a homeowners policy. In Myrtle Beach the line is the U.S. 17 Bypass. The owner then buys that cover separately, for example from the state's backup insurer for wind.</p><ul class="c3k-cards c3k-cards3" role="list" aria-label="Which side of the wind line each place is on"><li><p class="h"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11a9 7.5 0 0 1 18 0q-2.25-1.5-4.5 0t-4.5 0-4.5 0-4.5 0zM12 11v8M2.5 21.5c3-1.4 6.3-1.4 9.5 0s6.5 1.4 9.5 0"/></svg></span><span>On the ocean side</span></p><p>Most of the City of Myrtle Beach, Surfside Beach and Garden City.</p></li><li><p class="h"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span><span>West of the law's line</span></p><p>Carolina Forest, Socastee and Conway are west of it. The state has at times let its wind insurer write policies there too, so we check the address.</p></li><li><p class="h"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M12 21s-7-6.2-7-11.5a7 7 0 0 1 14 0C19 14.8 12 21 12 21z"/><circle cx="12" cy="9.5" r="2.5"/></svg></span><span>On both sides</span></p><p>North Myrtle Beach and Little River. It depends on the street. Murrells Inlet straddles the Horry and Georgetown county line. On the Georgetown side, the law draws its own line near U.S. 17.</p></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The $2,000 includes those wind policies. About 1 in 10 house owners here pay $4,000 or more a year for all their home insurance, and about 1 in 40 pay $6,000 or more. The Census figures do not split by side of the highway. Our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">coastal insurance guide</a> explains each policy.</p><div class="c3k-we"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12.5l4.2 4.2L19 7"/></svg></span><p>If you buy with us, we get you an insurance quote for the address before you make an offer, so wind and flood are in your budget before you commit.</p></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I need flood insurance in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You must have it if the home is in the high-risk flood zone and your loan is from a bank or other lender the federal government regulates. Anywhere else it is your choice, but since 2015, 3 in 10 paid flood claims in Horry County were on buildings outside that zone. The Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) maps the zone.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 1 in 15 houses resold in Horry County were in the zone, and about 1 in 4 condos.</p><p class="c3k-tl">A FEMA flood policy on a house, the typical cost a year:</p><div class="c3k-stat c3k-stat3"><div><span class="big">$550</span><span class="s">your home, outside the high-risk zone</span></div><div><span class="big">$1,070</span><span class="s">your home, inside the zone</span></div><div><span class="big">$1,370</span><span class="s">a second home, inside the zone</span></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A FEMA flood policy on a condo unit its owner lives in, outside the high-risk zone, costs about $375 a year. Flood is not in the yearly total above; you can add it in the calculator below. Our page on <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">homes that have flooded</a> shows how we learn a house's flood history.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="upkeep"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Upkeep">How much should I budget for upkeep?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About $3,270 a year for a house: about $1,470 of routine repairs and about $1,800 put toward the big replacements. A condo owner spends about $690 a year on upkeep; the building's upkeep is paid from the condo fee.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most years cost far less than the average, and some years cost much more. Half of house owners spend about $520 or less on routine repairs in a year, and about 1 in 6 spend nothing. The average is high because of the big jobs:</p><ul class="c3k-cards" role="list" aria-label="What the big replacements cost"><li><p class="h"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M2.5 12.5 12 4l9.5 8.5"/><path d="M5 10.5V20h14v-9.5"/><path d="M9.5 20v-5h5v5"/></svg></span><span>A new roof</span><span class="p">$10,400</span></p><p>About 1 in 9 owners replaced one in two years.</p></li><li><p class="h"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="16" rx="2"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4.5"/><path d="M12 7.5v9M7.5 12h9"/></svg></span><span>Central air conditioning</span><span class="p">$7,000</span></p><p>About 1 in 11 owners replaced it in two years.</p></li><li><p class="h"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M12 21c3.6 0 6-2.4 6-5.6 0-3.8-3.4-5.6-4.2-9.4-1.7 1.3-2.6 3-2.4 5-1.2-.6-2-1.8-2.2-3.2C7.3 9.6 6 12 6 15.4 6 18.6 8.4 21 12 21Z"/></svg></span><span>Heating equipment</span><span class="p">$5,000</span></p><p>The furnace or heat pump.</p></li><li><p class="h"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="6" y="3" width="12" height="18" rx="3"/><path d="M12 8.5c1.4 1.7 2.2 2.9 2.2 4a2.2 2.2 0 0 1-4.4 0c0-1.1.8-2.3 2.2-4Z"/><path d="M9 21v1M15 21v1"/></svg></span><span>A water heater</span><span class="p">$1,000</span></p></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Budgeting 1 percent of the value a year is a little high: on the $350,000 house it gives $3,500.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="utilities"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Utilities">What are electric and water bills like in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Electricity costs less here than in the rest of South Carolina, and water is a small bill. These are typical bills for owners:</p><div class="c3k-stat"><div><span class="big">$150</span><span class="s">a month for electricity on a house here</span></div><div><span class="big">$190</span><span class="s">a month in the rest of South Carolina</span></div><div><span class="big">$480</span><span class="s">a year for water and sewer on a house</span></div><div><span class="big">$90</span><span class="s">a month for electricity in a condo</span></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One household's bills: on one of our team's own houses, about 1,400 square feet on two floors, summer electricity is about $130 a month and water about $50.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="calculator"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Calculator">What will my home cost each month? Use the calculator</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Pick a house or a condo, your town and how you will use it. Insurance, the HOA fee and upkeep are filled in from this page, and you can change any of them. Add a flood policy if the home needs one. Your cost shows at once. Add your rate and down payment from your lender to see the loan payment.</p><div class="c3k-calc" id="c3k-calc"><h3 class="h3" style="margin-top:0">Your home</h3><div class="grid"><div class="full"><span style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">What are you buying?</span><div class="seg" role="radiogroup" aria-label="House or condo"><label><input type="radio" name="c3kType" value="house" checked><span>A house</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="c3kType" value="condo"><span>A condo</span></label></div></div><div class="full"><span style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">How will you use it?</span><div class="seg" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Your home or a second home"><label><input type="radio" name="c3kUse" value="4" checked><span>My home</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="c3kUse" value="6"><span>Second home</span></label></div></div><label for="c3kLoc" class="full">Where<small>Outside city limits: Carolina Forest, Socastee, Little River</small><select id="c3kLoc"><option value="201|0|89.4" selected>Outside city limits</option><option value="254.6|83.4|0">City of Myrtle Beach</option><option value="221.2|0|0">North Myrtle Beach</option><option value="214.2|0|82">Surfside Beach (in the town)</option><option value="272.5|0|0">Conway (in the city)</option><option value="207.3|0|89.4">Garden City and Murrells Inlet, Horry side</option></select></label><label for="c3kPrice">Price<input id="c3kPrice" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="5000" value="350000"></label><label for="c3kIns">Insurance, a year<small>What owners here pay</small><input id="c3kIns" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="50" value="2000"></label><label for="c3kHoa">HOA or condo fee, a month<small>A typical house for sale here</small><input id="c3kHoa" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="5" value="100"></label><label for="c3kUp">Upkeep, a year<small>The regional average</small><input id="c3kUp" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="50" value="3270"></label><label for="c3kFlood">Flood policy, a year<small>About $550 outside the high-risk zone, $1,070 inside it</small><input id="c3kFlood" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="10" value="0"></label></div><h3 class="h3">Your loan (optional)</h3><div class="grid"><label for="c3kDown">Down payment, percent<small>Your own figure</small><input id="c3kDown" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="100" step="1" placeholder="Yours"></label><label for="c3kRate">Rate, percent<small>From your lender's quote</small><input id="c3kRate" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.125" placeholder="Yours"></label><label for="c3kTerm">Term, years<input id="c3kTerm" type="number" inputmode="numeric" min="1" step="1" value="30"></label></div><h3 class="h3">What it costs</h3><div class="out" aria-live="polite"><div><span>Property tax</span><strong id="c3kTax"></strong></div><div><span>County fee on the tax bill</span><strong id="c3kFee"></strong></div><div><span>Insurance</span><strong id="c3kInsO"></strong></div><div><span>HOA or condo fee</span><strong id="c3kHoaO"></strong></div><div><span>Upkeep</span><strong id="c3kUpO"></strong></div><div><span>Flood policy</span><strong id="c3kFloodO"></strong></div><div class="tot"><span>Cost besides the loan<small id="c3kYear"></small></span><strong id="c3kBesides"></strong></div><div class="wait"><span>Loan payment</span><strong id="c3kPI"></strong></div><div class="tot"><span>Total a month with the loan</span><strong id="c3kAll"></strong></div></div></div><script>(function(){var D={"house":{"price":350000,"ins":2000,"hoa":100,"up":3270},"condo":{"price":215000,"ins":800,"hoa":650,"up":690}},FH={"house":"About $550 outside the high-risk zone, $1,070 inside it","condo":"About $375 for a unit outside the high-risk zone"},HH2={"house":"A typical house for sale here","condo":"About $650 county-wide; $425 in Carolina Forest"};var $=function(i){return document.getElementById(i)};var num=function(i){var v=parseFloat($(i).value);return isFinite(v)&&v>0?v:0};var m=function(n){return "$"+Math.round(n).toLocaleString("en-US")};var pick=function(n){var e=document.querySelector('input[name="'+n+'"]:checked');return e?e.value:""};function calc(){if(!$("c3kTax"))return;var p=num("c3kPrice"),L=$("c3kLoc").value.split("|"),lev=+L[0],city=+L[1],fee=+L[2],six=pick("c3kUse")==="6";var tax=six?p*.06*lev/1000:Math.max(0,p*.04*(lev-109.1)/1000-p*.04*city/1000*.534);var ins=num("c3kIns"),hoa=num("c3kHoa")*12,up=num("c3kUp"),fl=num("c3kFlood");var yr=tax+fee+ins+hoa+up+fl;$("c3kTax").textContent=m(tax/12)+" a month";$("c3kFee").textContent=fee?m(fee/12)+" a month":"none";$("c3kInsO").textContent=m(ins/12)+" a month";$("c3kHoaO").textContent=m(hoa/12)+" a month";$("c3kUpO").textContent=m(up/12)+" a month";$("c3kFloodO").textContent=fl?m(fl/12)+" a month":"none";$("c3kBesides").textContent=m(yr/12)+" a month";$("c3kYear").textContent=m(yr)+" a year";var hd=$("c3kDown").value.trim()!=="",hr=$("c3kRate").value.trim()!==""&&parseFloat($("c3kRate").value)>=0;if(!(hd&&hr)){var ask="Add "+(hd?"your rate":hr?"your down payment":"your down payment and rate");$("c3kPI").textContent=ask;$("c3kAll").textContent="--";return}var loan=p*(1-Math.min(100,parseFloat($("c3kDown").value)||0)/100),r=Math.max(0,parseFloat($("c3kRate").value)||0)/1200,n=(num("c3kTerm")||30)*12;var pi=r>0?loan*r*Math.pow(1+r,n)/(Math.pow(1+r,n)-1):loan/n;$("c3kPI").textContent=m(pi)+" a month";$("c3kAll").textContent=m(pi+yr/12)+" a month"}function type(){var d=D[pick("c3kType")]||D.house;var pv=+$("c3kPrice").value;if(pv===D.house.price||pv===D.condo.price)$("c3kPrice").value=d.price;$("c3kIns").value=d.ins;$("c3kHoa").value=d.hoa;$("c3kUp").value=d.up;var c=pick("c3kType")==="condo";$("c3kHoa").previousElementSibling.textContent=c?HH2.condo:HH2.house;$("c3kFlood").previousElementSibling.textContent=c?FH.condo:FH.house;calc()}function go(){var c=$("c3k-calc");if(!c)return;c.addEventListener("input",calc);c.addEventListener("change",function(e){if(e.target.name==="c3kType")type();else calc()});calc()}if(document.readyState==="loading")document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",go);else go()})();</script><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The calculator uses Horry County's current rates, the same math as our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax calculator</a>. It leaves out the 65 and older exemption and small special districts.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="cost-to-own-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Cost to own FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is PITI, and what is not in it?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">PITI stands for principal, interest, taxes and insurance. It is the loan payment plus the property tax and home insurance, which the lender usually collects with it, and mortgage insurance if the loan needs it. An HOA or condo fee is usually paid separately. Upkeep and utilities are not in it.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Will my property tax be the same as the seller's?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not always. After a sale, the county values the home again, and the new value is first taxed the year after the sale. Do not budget from the seller's tax bill.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does the cost change if I rent the home out?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. A rental is taxed like a second home, on 6 percent of its value. The second-home section above shows the tax by town.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is Myrtle Beach expensive to own a home in?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A little more than the rest of South Carolina. Owners of a paid-off house here pay about $50 a month more for tax, insurance, utilities and any HOA fee together.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/kufln4qp/tax-levy-2026_2.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2026 county levy</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/stormwater/major-initiatives/utility-fee/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stormwater fee</a>, <a href="https://www.cityofmyrtlebeach.com/departments/financial_management_and_reporting.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Myrtle Beach budget</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c037.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">12-37</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t38c075.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">38-75</a>, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/4012a" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">42 U.S.C. 4012a</a>, <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/microdata.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census ACS</a>, <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/ahs.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AHS</a>, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/openfema-data-page/nfip-redacted-policies-v3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEMA policies</a>, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/openfema-data-page/fima-nfip-redacted-claims-v2" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEMA claims</a>, <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/on-a-mortgage-whats-the-difference-between-my-principal-and-interest-payment-and-my-total-monthly-payment-en-1941/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB</a>. Prices: county deeds, 12 months to September 2026. Insurance, utilities, HOA share: Census American Community Survey 2024, Horry County outside the Loris area; insurance includes wind and flood where carried. Upkeep: Census American Housing Survey 2023, South Atlantic states. Fees: homes for sale, Horry ZIPs, one day. Not legal, tax or insurance advice.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Houses on streets in Carolina Forest, outside city limits, seen from above. Photo: NOAA National Geodetic Survey, <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://noaa-eri-pds.s3.amazonaws.com/index.html#2026_Pre_Event/EC2601a_OB_N_RGB/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA Emergency Response Imagery</a>. Chapter3 Realty cut a 1080 x 810 px view from one NOAA map tile, raised the contrast and color slightly, and resized it to 1200 x 900.</div><div role="listitem">Condo buildings on the ocean in Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, 2011, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped and resized the photo.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/carolina-forest-house-streets-from-above-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/buyers-cost-to-own/carolina-forest-house-streets-from-above-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":900,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Houses on streets in Carolina Forest, outside city limits, seen from above","caption":"Houses on streets in Carolina Forest, outside city limits, seen from above. The typical house in the table above is one like these.","description":"Rows of two-story houses with gray roofs and short driveways along curving streets and a cul-de-sac, beside ponds and pine woods, seen from above","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"NOAA National Geodetic Survey"},"creditText":"NOAA National Geodetic Survey, public domain","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (NOAA National Geodetic Survey)","license":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm","acquireLicensePage":"https://noaa-eri-pds.s3.ama