>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="retiring-to-myrtle-beach-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Retiring to Myrtle Beach FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does a retired couple have to file a South Carolina tax return?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A married couple, both 65 or older, does not have to file a South Carolina return unless their South Carolina income is over $60,000.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How do I apply for the homestead exemption in Horry County?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Apply at the Horry County Auditor's office, and inside a city at the city too, by July 15 for that year's bill. You must own the home and live in it as your legal home. The exemption takes the first $50,000 of the home's value off the tax.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I lose the 4 percent rate if I rent the home out?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only if you rent it out for more than 72 days in a calendar year. State law keeps the 4 percent rate on a home you live in as long as it is not rented for more than 72 days in that year.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What makes a community 55+ under federal law?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">To count as 55+ housing under federal fair housing law