<span><strong>89%</strong><span class="t">of one South Carolina HOA's court bill was lawyers' fees and court costs</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 2, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">How an association forecloses, how often it happens in Horry County, and the steps that stop it before the sale.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. For a house or a condo, a South Carolina homeowners association (HOA) or condo association can put a lien on your home for unpaid dues and ask a court to sell it. The lien is on the home itself, so it applies whether or not you have a mortgage.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The bill that starts it can be small. Lawyers' fees and court costs are added to it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you are sued, you usually have 30 days to answer; the summons gives the time. Answer, and get the case itself dismissed, because paying the bill does not by itself end the lawsuit. Or sell before the auction.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Behind on HOA dues? Ask us about selling before the auction.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" 