-</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most rented houses here have three bedrooms: about six in ten. Houses are about three in ten rented homes. About half are apartments and condos, and about one in seven is a mobile home.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Middle rent a month, leases old and new, most of Horry County"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Middle rent a month, leases old and new, most of Horry County</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Kind of home, utilities the renter pays</span><span role="columnheader">rent before utilities</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">1-bedroom apartment or condo</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">plus about $100 of utilities</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.612" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1,100</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2-bedroom apartment or condo</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">plus about $100 of utilities</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.644" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1,150</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">3-bedroom house</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">plus about $200 of utilities</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.833" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1,500</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Census surveys of renters.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The chart counts every lease in place, old and new, so each figure is lower than what a new tenant pays. The survey has too few rented houses with two or four bedrooms for a firm figure. Condos rented by the night are another market: <a href="/invest/condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our condo investing guide</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="new-leases"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="New leases">Why does a new lease cost more than the rent the last tenant paid?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Across every rented home, a lease signed in the last two years costs about 40 percent more than one signed five or more years ago. For rented houses, it is about $1,600 a month against about $1,100, before utilities.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Middle rent a month before utilities, by how long the renter has been in the home"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Middle rent a month before utilities, by how long the renter has been in the home</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Renters</span><span role="columnheader">rent before utilities</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Rented houses: moved in within 2 years</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.893" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1,600</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Rented houses: stayed 5 years or more</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.596" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1,100</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every rented home: moved in within 2 years</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.743" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1,340</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every rented home: stayed 5 years or more</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.514" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$930</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Census surveys of renters, most of Horry County.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">An average of all leases includes long tenants on old rents, so it is lower than what the next tenant will pay.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For an owner, it means two things. Plan a house you buy empty on the new-lease figure. A house you buy with a tenant in it may come with an older, lower rent until that lease ends. Before you offer, we find out how long the tenant has been there and what the lease says.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="by-area"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="By area">What does rent cost in Carolina Forest, Socastee and the rest of the Grand Strand?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Asking rent is the rent on a home listed for rent now. Across ten Grand Strand ZIP codes, it is lowest in the north end of Myrtle Beach, about $1,530 a month, and highest in North Myrtle Beach, about $1,950. The asking rent in the highest ZIP code is about $420 a month more than in the lowest.</p><figure style="margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:760px"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" id="c3r-where-the-places-in-the-" viewBox="0 0 360 306" width="360" height="306" role="img" aria-label="Where the places in the rent table are: a map of 9 places, numbered 1, North Myrtle Beach; 2, Murrells Inlet and Garden City; 3, Longs; 4, Conway; 5, Carolina Forest and Forestbrook; 6, Myrtle Beach; 7, Socastee; 8, Surfside Beach; and 9, Little River." style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:760px;background:#f7f3ec;border:1px solid rg